  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 240 PM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Radar showers associated with our cold front are quickly \nexiting our eastern cwa this aftn, as mid lvl GOES-19 water vapor \nimagery is showing drier air aloft rapidly expanding acrs our fa. \nRainfall amounts ranged from 0.50 to 0.75\" acrs northern NY to 0.25 \nto 0.50\" CPV to 0.05 to 0.25\" acrs most of central/eastern VT today. \nGradient btwn building 1025mb high pres to our west and departing \n1007mb low pres over central ME, should limit fog development until \nsunrise on Sat. Upstream vis satl imagery shows immediate clearing \napproaching the SLV, but this axis is narrow with plenty of stratus \ncumulus clouds behind clearing axis. So anticipating areas of clouds \novernight, especially in the trrn given caa and favorable upslope \nflow. Lows with clouds/winds hold in the mid/upper 30s to mid/upper \n40s. For Saturday soundings show deep dry layer aloft building \ndirectly overhead, but still notice some moisture trapped below \nsubsidence inversion around 925mb, which combined with upslope flow \nand lingering/leftover bl moisture from recent rainfall wl produce \nareas of fair wx cumulus clouds, especially trrn. Did use the mtn \nmax temp tool to sharpen temp range btwn valleys and summits on \nSaturday with highs l/m 40s summits to lower 60s warmest valleys. \n\nA 1029mb high pres is located over the NEK of VT at 06z Sunday, \nwhich should result in mostly clear skies and light winds, allowing \nfor fog development and areas of frost. Lows range from the mid 20s \nNEK/SLK to lower 40s near Lake Champlain by Sunday morning.\n\nNext system has vigorous s/w energy with digging mid/upper lvl trof, \nbut deep layer moisture is limited as pw values are in the 0.60\" to \n0.75\" ahead of boundary. Several broken line of low top showers are \nlikely late Sunday into Monday associated with mid/upper lvl trof \nand multiple boundaries moving acrs our cwa. Winds shift to the \nwest/northwest by Monday aftn with sharply falling temps, especially \nsummits as progged 850mb temps fall below 0C. Would not be surprised \na few frozen pellets/graupel possible on Monday aftn, given thermal \nprofiles, but moisture is quickly waning as temps are cool enough to \nsupport frozen precip. Best probability would be above 3500 feet \nafter 18z on Monday, given crnt sounding data at Jay Peak.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Strong northwest flow will advect plenty of cold air \nMonday night into Tuesday, with temperatures falling into the mid \n20s to mid 30s, though areas near Lake Champlain will likely hover \naround 40. Freezing levels will fall well below summit levels, but \ndry air will infiltrate rapidly behind the front so no snow is \nexpected. There could be a few lingering clouds to cause a bit of \nrime ice but even the low clouds look to scour out fast. There \nshould be some level of flow lingering through Monday night into \nTuesday that should keep frost from developing. However, higher \nelevations will simply go straight to freezing from the advection. \nFall will very much be in the air on Tuesday with brisk winds and \nhigh temperatures lingering in the mid 40s to mid 50s. Then on \nTuesday night, broad surface high pressure will set up. It looks \nlike we'll also be around the PWAT min across the region, and so \nvery efficient radiational cooling is increasing likely. Almost the \nentire region could experience a freeze, and the Champlain Valley \nremains in the alert window for this time. So this will likely be \ncurtain call for all gardener's sensitive plants in the broad \nvalleys for Tuesday night. Surface high pressure will amble east \nafter Wednesday, and temperatures will start moderating. The next \nfront will arrive next Friday with some scattered light showers.",
